---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { ISSUES_URL, LICENSE_NAME, LICENSE_URL, REPO_URL, appleMark, githubMark } from '../lib/project';
import { RELEASES_PAGE, releaseState, releaseView } from '../lib/release';
const release = releaseView(await releaseState(), 'macOS 13 or newer', 'Apple Silicon and Intel');
const checksumHref = release.version ? `${RELEASES_PAGE}/download/${release.version}/SHA256SUMS` : `${RELEASES_PAGE}/latest/download/SHA256SUMS`;
---
<SiteLayout title="Download" description="Download Sift for macOS: a native Gmail client with no bundled browser engine." path="/download">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Download Sift</h1>
    <p>{release.facts.join(' · ')}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card-block" style="display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href={release.href}><span set:html={appleMark} />{release.label}</a>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={REPO_URL}><span class="btn-mark" set:html={githubMark} />View source</a>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={RELEASES_PAGE}>All releases</a>
    <p class="install-note">Not notarized yet. <a href="#first-launch">Read the one-time opening steps ↓</a></p>
    <span class="pill-row">
      {release.size && <span class="chip">{release.size}</span>}
      <span class="chip">universal build</span>
      <span class="chip">free beta</span>
      <span class="chip">{LICENSE_NAME} licensed</span>
    </span>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    {!release.published && <p class="metric-foot">{release.status}</p>}

    <h2 id="homebrew">More of a Terminal person?</h2>
    <p>With <a href="https://brew.sh">Homebrew</a> installed, one command downloads the verified release and puts Sift in Applications:</p>
    <pre class="install-command"><code>brew install --cask aadixc0de/tap/sift</code></pre>
    <p>Homebrew handles installation and checks the download’s checksum. Apple’s first-launch check still applies — follow the steps below if it appears.</p>

    <h2 id="first-launch">One tiny macOS detour.</h2>
    <p>Sift is a small indie app without Apple’s notarization stamp yet. The inbox is ready; the Apple paperwork isn’t. macOS may show “Apple could not verify Sift.app.” Here’s what to do.</p>
    <h3>First, move in.</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the downloaded DMG and drag <strong>Sift</strong> into Applications.</li>
      <li>Open Sift from <strong>Applications</strong>. If macOS shows the verification warning, dismiss it.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll down, and choose <strong>Open Anyway</strong> beside Sift. Confirm and authenticate if asked.</li>
      <li>Connect your Gmail with an app password — no Google Cloud project, no consent screen.</li>
    </ol>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>No “Open Anyway”? Take the side door.</summary>
      <p>If you downloaded Sift from this page, compare the DMG with the SHA-256 checksum below first. Then open Terminal and paste:</p>
      <pre class="install-command"><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Sift.app</code></pre>
      <p>Now open Sift from Applications. This removes the quarantine flag from Sift only; it does not turn off Gatekeeper or change your Mac’s global security settings. It also does not mean Apple has reviewed the app.</p>
    </details>
    <p class="metric-foot">The warning is expected for this unnotarized build. <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-gb/102445">Apple’s guide to opening downloaded apps</a>.</p>

    <h2>Connect Gmail</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><span class="step-n">1</span><p>Turn on 2-Step Verification in your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/signinoptions/two-step-verification">Google account settings</a>.</p></li>
      <li><span class="step-n">2</span><p>Create an app password named Sift on the <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords">App passwords page</a>. Google shows the 16 letter password once.</p></li>
      <li><span class="step-n">3</span><p>Paste it into Sift and press Connect.</p></li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Checksums</h2>
    {
      release.sha && (
        <p>
          SHA-256 of the DMG published in {release.version}: <code>{release.sha}</code> — read from that
          release's <code>SHA256SUMS</code> asset rather than typed by hand.
        </p>
      )
    }
    <p><a href={checksumHref}>Download the published SHA256SUMS file</a> to compare with your installer.</p>
    <p>The release pipeline generates <code>SHA256SUMS</code> from the exact bytes it uploads, then re-downloads the uploaded file and verifies it while the release is still a draft. Nothing is published until that check passes, so the hash and the file cannot disagree.</p>

    <h2 id="source">Source and license</h2>
    <p>Sift is open source under the <a href={LICENSE_URL}>{LICENSE_NAME} License</a>. The whole app — the Rust sync engine, the React interface, the release pipeline and this website — lives in one public repository, so you can read exactly what the build you install does with your mail, or compile it yourself.</p>
    <p>Bug reports and feature requests go to <a href={ISSUES_URL}>GitHub Issues</a>. The build steps are in the repository’s README; a local build needs macOS, Node 20+, pnpm 9+ and a stable Rust toolchain.</p>

    <h2>Updates</h2>
    <p>Sift checks GitHub for a newer release when it launches — once per launch, only when you are online — and you can turn that check off or run it by hand in <strong>Settings → Updates</strong>. Nothing downloads or installs on its own: the Updates tab shows the new version, its date and its release notes, and then you press <strong>Download update</strong>, then <strong>Install and quit to finish</strong>, then <strong>Restart Sift now</strong>. The download is verified against the public key built into the app before anything is installed; a download that fails that check is rejected, and the tab says the update could not be verified.</p>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
